-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses a 2Q eviction policy to protect comparison sheets during sequential screenings. 2Q maintains two queues: a FIFO first-use queue (A1in) and an LRU reuse queue (Am). New cache entries join the first-use queue; when evicted from there, an entry is remembered in a ghost list. If a second access arrives while the entry remains in the ghost list, the re-rendered entry joins the reuse queue instead. This structure isolates one-off screening scans (which exhaust the first-use queue) from repeatedly compared clips (which stabilize in the reuse queue).
+
+The first-use queue holds up to 25% of the cache capacity; the reuse queue holds the remaining 75%. When admission would exceed the 12 GiB total, the eviction order is:
+
+1. Evict from the first-use queue's head (oldest entry) if that queue is non-empty.
+2. Otherwise, evict from the reuse queue via LRU.
+
+A cache hit on a first-use entry promotes it immediately to the reuse queue. A cache hit on a reuse entry refreshes its LRU position. The ghost list is bounded to the same size as the first-use queue and itself uses FIFO replacement.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (separate queues for first-use and reused entries) | LRU (simple but vulnerable to sequential scans), Frequency-based with aging (protects popular clips but adds counter maintenance and lags on project switches) | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of small clip sets. 2Q protects reused sheets from one-off screening scans by isolating them in the reuse queue, accepting extra bookkeeping (two queue pointers and a ghost list) to preserve comparison workflows during screenings. See [decisions/eviction-policy.md](decisions/eviction-policy.md) for full analysis. |
